  "sourceUrl": "https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/8.0.2/hardware-acceleration/793766/fortigate-200g-and-201g-fast-path-architecture",
  "notes": "Fortinet FortiGate 200G and 201G fast path architecture: \"Two 10/100/1000BASE-T RJ45 (HA, MGMT) that are not connected to the NP7Lite. Eight 10/100/1000BASE-T RJ45 (1 to 8). Eight 5GigE/2.5GigE/1GigE/100M BASE-T RJ45 interfaces (9 to 16). Eight 10/1 GigE SFP+/SFP (X1 to X8). X1 and X2 are FortiLink interfaces. Four 1GigE SFP (17 to 20).\" The same port list appears in `diagnose npu np7lite port-list`. The FG-200G-DAT-R12 data sheet agrees: 8 x GE RJ45, 1 x GE RJ45 MGMT, 1 x GE RJ45 HA, 8 x 5/2.5/GE RJ45, 4 x GE SFP slots, 8 x 10/GE SFP+/SFP FortiLink slots. port1-8 are the eight 10/100/1000BASE-T copper switch ports (issue #59); they used to be generated by a portTemplate that no spec.interfaces-based audit could see, and carried no connector or rate list. port9-16 are declared 100M/1G/2.5G/5G rather than the generic 10M-5G ladder because neither source lists 10BASE-T on those ports. FortiOS assigns wan1/wan2 in configuration; there is no WAN silk-screen on the chassis."
